  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A phage-encoded RNA-binding protein inhibits the antiviral activity of a toxin\u2013antitoxin system (2023) in Nucleic Acids Research. Funding: Life Sciences Research Foundation, NSF, Howard Hughes Medical Institute Gilliam Fellowship, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Merck Fellow of the Jane Coffin Childs Fund for Medical Research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A phage-encoded RNA-binding protein inhibits the antiviral activity of a toxin\u2013antitoxin system (2023) in Nucleic Acids Research is authored by Chantal K. Guegler, Gabriella I. C. Teodoro and Sriram Srikant. Topics: Biology, Antitoxin, Endoribonuclease, RNA and Ribonucleoprotein.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">8 citations (OpenAlex). Citations peaked in 2025 with 5 citing works. Citing venues include Journal of Molecular Biology, Wiley Interdisciplinary Reviews - RNA and Scientific Reports.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 Nucleic Acids Research article is already showing early scholarly uptake: OpenAlex records 8 citations and the reuse graph documents 9 downstream reuses, including articles, a review, a preprint, and a biomedical article mention. The paper\u2019s most visible contribution is mechanistic, clarifying how the phage-encoded RNA-binding protein TifA inhibits the bacterial toxIN toxin-antitoxin defense system.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Guegler and colleagues address a central problem in phage\u2013bacteria conflict: how bacteriophages overcome bacterial immune defenses. Focusing on T4-like phages, the study explains an unresolved mechanism behind tifA, a phage gene known to block the type III toxin-antitoxin system toxIN. The authors show that TifA directly binds both the ToxN endoribonuclease and RNA, supporting a model in which phage resistance suppression depends on a specific RNA-binding interaction rather than a generic inhibitory effect.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s impact is still early, but the downstream record is already informative. Citations are concentrated in the years after publication and appear in venues that sit close to the paper\u2019s subject area, including Journal of Molecular Biology, EcoSal Plus, Microbiology Spectrum, Scientific Reports, Viruses, and a review in Wiley Interdisciplinary Reviews - RNA. The citing literature is also thematically aligned, with bacteriophage\u2013microbe interactions, toxin-antitoxin systems, and RNA biology recurring across the landscape.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse signals reinforce that this work is being taken up as part of a broader research conversation rather than as a one-off reference. The reuse graph captures six articles, one review, one preprint, and one biomedical article mention, suggesting that the study is being used in both primary research and synthesis. The citation landscape also spans multiple countries and institutions, indicating a geographically distributed uptake, with especially visible activity from education-sector organizations.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication context further supports accessibility and dissemination. It was published open access in Nucleic Acids Research, and repository records show the article is openly available. Funding from the Life Sciences Research Foundation, NSF, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, the HHMI Gilliam Fellowship, and the Jane Coffin Childs Fund points to substantial support for the work, while the available record does not yet show broader public-event attention. Overall, the evidence points to a focused but meaningful early influence in phage biology, RNA binding, and toxin-antitoxin system research.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
